		{
			"item": "letter:v",
			"display": "В в",
			"clue": "v, as in van",
			"note": "вода́: water. Often softer than an English v, and like w at the end of a syllable, as in був (was).",
			"verdict": "ok",
			"comment": "Judgement call, not an error. Wikipedia (Ukrainian phonology): в is /ʋ/, a labiodental approximant, realised as [ʋ], [β] or [w], most often [w]/[u̯] in syllable codas and word-initially before a consonant; Wiktionary gives був as [buu̯] (final в = non-syllabic u), so \"like w at the end of a syllable, as in був\" is supported, and був = masculine past of бути \"to be\" so \"was\" is right. \"Softer than an English v\" is loose (the sound is less buzzy than English v, but sources do not use \"softer\"; palatalised /ʋʲ/ is rare); acceptable as a hint. Note that Wiktionary transcribes вода as [wɔˈda], so the initial в there is often w-like too, but \"often\" covers this."
		},
		{
			"item": "letter:y",
			"display": "И и",
			"clue": "y, like i in bit",
			"note": "ри́ба: fish. Like the i in bit, never the ee sound, which is і.",
			"verdict": "ok",
			"comment": "Wikipedia (Ukrainian phonology): и is /ɪ/ (Wikipedia Ukrainian alphabet article: close central /ɪ̈/, like i in mitt) and і is /i/. Wiktionary: ри́ба [ˈrɪbɐ], \"fish\". Clue and note are accurate; \"never the ee sound\" is a fair teaching simplification (Ukrainian /ɪ/ is somewhat tenser and more fronted than English /ɪ/)."
		},
